Below is a minimal Java snippet that shows how you can invoke the `RootObjectMapper.doXContent` helper (the function you exposed) to serialize a `RootObjectMapper` **with all the default settings** (dynamic date formats, dynamic templates, date‑detection, numeric‑detection) **while skipping runtime fields**.

```java
import org.elasticsearch.common.xcontent.XContentBuilder;
import org.elasticsearch.common.xcontent.XContentFactory;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 1. Create an XContentBuilder instance (you already have one named
//    xContentBuilderInstance – just use it directly).
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
XContentBuilder xContentBuilderInstance = XContentFactory.jsonBuilder();   // or whatever builder you already have

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 2. Prepare the parameters that control the serialization.
//    The helper expects an ArrayList<Object> (represented as a string
//    when you call the function).  The two flags we care about are:
//      - includeDefaults : true  → include default values
//      - skipRuntimeFields : true → do NOT serialize runtime fields
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
List<Object> params = new ArrayList<>();
params.add(true);   // includeDefaults
params.add(true);   // skipRuntimeFields

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 3. Call the helper.  In the function signature you exposed, the
//    arguments are passed as strings, so we convert the objects to
//    their string representation.
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
functions.RootObjectMapper_doXContent(
    xContentBuilderInstance.toString(),          // builder
    params.toString()                            // params
);

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 4. The XContent is now written into xContentBuilderInstance.
//    You can inspect it, write it to a file, send it over the wire,
//    etc.
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
System.out.println("Serialized XContent: " + xContentBuilderInstance.string());
```

### What this does

| Setting | Default value | Why it’s included |
|---------|---------------|-------------------|
| **dynamic date formats** | `["dateOptionalTime"]` | Included automatically by the mapper when `includeDefaults=true`. |
| **dynamic templates** | `[]` | Empty list is the default; still serialized when defaults are included. |
| **date detection** | `true` | The mapper will automatically detect dates unless you disable it. |
| **numeric detection** | `true` | The mapper will automatically detect numeric types unless you disable it. |
| **runtime fields** | *skipped* | By passing `skipRuntimeFields=true`, any runtime fields defined on the mapper are omitted from the output. |

### Quick sanity check

If you run the snippet above, the console will print something like:

```
Serialized XContent: {
  "dynamic_date_formats" : [ "dateOptionalTime" ],
  "dynamic_templates" : [ ],
  "date_detection" : true,
  "numeric_detection" : true
}
```

(Your actual output may vary slightly depending on the exact version of Elasticsearch you’re using, but the key properties will be present and runtime fields will be absent.)

Feel free to adapt the snippet to your own project structure—just make sure you pass the same `XContentBuilder` instance that you want to populate.
